-- title:
--   A k₀-rational level field inside the full-level function field
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q \ge 5$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, let $k_0$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\zeta_q \in k_0$ be an element whose image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity. Write $F =$ `fieldBar q M'` for the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ obtained by base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the function field `xHFunctionField` of level $q^2M'$ with $H =$ `levelH q M'` the kernel of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, and give $k_0$ the algebra structure over which $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to F$. The assertion is that there is an intermediate field $F_0$ of $F$ over $k_0$ with the following four properties. First, $F_0$ is exactly the set of $f \in F$ all of whose Laurent coefficients $f_n$ ($n \in \mathbb{Z}$) lie in the image of $k_0$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Second, the join of $F_0$ with the $k_0$-subfield generated by the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in $F$ is all of $F$, i.e. $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \cdot F_0 = F$. Third, $F_0$ is stable under all level automorphisms: for every $\zeta$ in `Idx q`, the set of primitive $q$-th roots of unity in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism `levelAutBar q M' ζ γ` of $F$ — the automorphism characterised by its effect on quotients of $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$ under the slash action of `conjElem q γ` along an embedding $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$, and taken to be the identity if no such automorphism exists — maps $F_0$ into $F_0$. Fourth, for every intermediate field $K'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $k_0$ with $K'/k_0$ finite, every $m$, every $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ that is linearly independent over $K'$, and every family $a_i$ of elements of the join of $F_0$ with the $k_0$-subfield of $F$ generated by the image of $K'$, the relation $\sum_i c_i a_i = 0$ forces $a_i = 0$ for all $i$; that is, $K' \cdot F_0$ and $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are linearly disjoint over $K'$.
--
--   This produces the $k_0$-rational form of the geometric function field of the full-level modular curve of level $K(q)K_0(M')$, the field of modular functions whose Fourier coefficients lie in $k_0 \supseteq \mathbb{Q}(\zeta_q)$, together with the three properties needed to descend the curve and its automorphisms to $k_0$: that it is a form ($\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \cdot F_0 = F$), that the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserve it, and that it stays regular after finite constant extensions. It is used in the construction of the descent base for the semistable model and in the analysis of affine charts at supersingular points.
-- source:
